<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taiping Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1850–1864</span> · Also known as Taiping</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Taiping Rebellion was a massive civil war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> between 1850 and 1864, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-xiuquan">Hong Xiuquan</a>, who proclaimed a Heavenly Kingdom of Great Peace inspired by his own version of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. One of the deadliest conflicts in history, it caused tens of millions of deaths and devastated central and southern China. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> profile treats it as a turning point that weakened dynastic authority and shifted military power to regional armies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taiping-rebellion.jpg" alt="Taiping Rebellion" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wu Youru · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Regaining_the_Provincial_City_Anqing2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
